<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.9" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="51847-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation + Plan note"/>
    <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Post-frostbite neuropathic pain of the fingers, well controlled (M79.2)</content>. Average pain 2 of 10 on duloxetine 60 mg, without drowsiness; blood pressure 128/80, back near his baseline. [S]<list><item>Continue duloxetine 60 mg daily; carry the whole supply when travelling and never stop it suddenly.</item><item>Capsaicin cream on cold days.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Right ankle sprain, recovering (S93.401A)</content>. Walking normally; balance good. [S]<list><item>Brace on rough ground and ice for another four weeks; continue balance exercises.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Risks of an Arctic air search flight</content>. A risk discussion, at his request; the decision is his. ECG today: sinus rhythm, no abnormality. No sign of heart disease on history and examination; blood pressure at target. The main medical risks are environmental: cold-water immersion after any forced landing on open water, with cold shock in the first minutes and hypothermia later; and cold injury, to which his previously frostbitten fingers are more vulnerable. His old shoulder fracture limits overhead reach, which matters for climbing out of an aircraft or into a raft. Stopping duloxetine suddenly would cause dizziness and other withdrawal effects. [S]<list><item>An immersion suit and life jacket worn over the water, with a survival kit, signalling equipment, and dry insulated clothing.</item><item>Practise the exit from the aircraft and boarding the raft with the shoulder&apos;s limits in mind.</item><item>Heated gloves, spare mittens, hand warmers; rewarm frostbitten skin only when it can be kept from refreezing; insulate and handle any hypothermic person gently.</item><item>Carry the medical summary, the advance care plan, a full supply of duloxetine, and capsaicin cream.</item><item>Second recombinant zoster vaccine dose given today.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Protocol monitoring: items the monitoring protocol calls for at this visit. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Domain</th><th>Measure</th><th>Interval</th><th>Status</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Pain severity (0 to 10 numeric rating) and overall improvement</td><td>Every review, and early after starting or changing a medicine</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Average 2 of 10</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Adverse effects, especially drowsiness, dizziness and nausea</td><td>Every review, and early after starting or changing a medicine</td><td>Completed today</td><td>No drowsiness or dizziness</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Daily activities and participation: fine hand tasks in the cold, driving, flying</td><td>Every review, and early after starting or changing a medicine</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Fine hand tasks manageable with gloves; driving; asked about flying</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Mood (PHQ-9) and anxiety</td><td>Every review, and early after starting or changing a medicine</td><td>Up to date</td><td>PHQ-9 3 five weeks ago; mood good by interview</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Sleep quality</td><td>Every review, and early after starting or changing a medicine</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Sleeping well</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Blood pressure</td><td>Before starting duloxetine and periodically on it</td><td>Completed today</td><td>128/80 mmHg</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Sodium and liver enzyme (ALT) on duloxetine</td><td>Baseline, then once in the first weeks of treatment</td><td>Up to date</td><td>Normal five weeks ago</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Interactions: no tramadol or other serotonergic drugs; caution with oral NSAIDs; alcohol</td><td>Every review</td><td>Completed today</td><td>No tramadol after the sprain; diclofenac gel only, now stopped</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Continuation and withdrawal: no abrupt stopping; taper if stopping</td><td>Every review, and before travel</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Full supply to carry; never stop suddenly</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Skin of the fingers and cold protection</td><td>Every review, and early after starting or changing a medicine</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Skin intact; cold protection plan for the flight</td></tr></tbody></table><paragraph>Goal progress:</paragraph><list><item>Average finger pain at or below 3 of 10, with fewer cold attacks, by four months. Outcome by 1928-06-09: Average pain 2 of 10 on duloxetine 60 mg; cold attacks shorter and less frequent [S]</item><item>Able to handle buttons, keys and a pen outdoors in the cold with protective gloves. Outcome by 1928-05-05: Managing with liner and heated gloves and hand warmers [S]</item><item>Any medicine used causes no daytime drowsiness that affects driving or flying. Outcome by 1928-06-09: No drowsiness on capsaicin cream or duloxetine [S]</item></list><paragraph>Immunizations given today: Recombinant zoster vaccine, dose 2 of 2 [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Guideline basis: Neuropathic pain in adults: pharmacological management in non-specialist settings (CG173) (2013, updated 2020); Wilderness Medical Society Clinical Practice Guidelines for the Prevention and Treatment of Frostbite: 2024 Update (McIntosh SE, et al. Wilderness Environ Med 2024;35(2):183-197) (2024); Wilderness Medical Society Clinical Practice Guidelines for the Out-of-Hospital Evaluation and Treatment of Accidental Hypothermia: 2019 Update (Dow J, et al. Wilderness Environ Med 2019;30(4S):S47-S69) (2019); Diagnosis, treatment and prevention of ankle sprains: update of an evidence-based clinical guideline (Vuurberg G, et al. Br J Sports Med 2018;52(15):956) (2018)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Review in three months, sooner after any journey. A written summary of the flight risk discussion was given to him. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
